.TH HD-DESKTOP-RENDER 8 "October 2026" "hd-desktop" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-desktop-render \- write the greetd config that boots into Hyprland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-desktop-render
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run | \-\-print\-user ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-desktop-render
writes
.IR /etc/greetd/config.toml ,
with which greetd logs a user in to Hyprland on tty1 at the boot, from the
template
.IR /usr/share/hd-desktop/templates/greetd.toml.j2 .
It gives
.IR /var/log/hyprland.log ,
where the output of Hyprland goes, to that user.
.PP
The installation of
.BR hd-desktop (7)
runs it, and
.B greetd.service
runs it before every start, by the drop-in
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/greetd.service.d/hd-desktop.conf .
A user that is made after the installation, as by the installer, is thus
logged in from the next boot on.
When it fails, greetd starts with the config it has.
.PP
Run it as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ,\  \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-n ,\  \-\-dry\-run
Show which user would be logged in and the config that would be written,
change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-user
Print the user that would be logged in, and nothing when nobody would be.
The installation of
.BR hd-desktop (7)
uses it to find the user who gets the files of
.IR /etc/skel .
It also works as a normal user.
.SH SETTINGS
The settings are the key
.B hd-desktop
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
see
.BR hd-config (7).
.TP
.B user
The user to log in.
Without it, the user account with the lowest UID from
.B UID_MIN
to
.B UID_MAX
of
.IR /etc/login.defs ,
the first user that was made.
.PP
Without such an account, or when the user of the setting does not exist,
greetd logs nobody in: it starts the console on tty1 and stops.
For example:
.PP
.RS
sudo hd-config-update hd-desktop '{"user": "alice"}'
.br
sudo hd-desktop-render
.RE
.PP
The change takes effect at the next boot.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the config was written or is up to date, 1 on an error and 2 on wrong
arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-desktop (7),
.BR hd-config (7),
.BR greetd (1)
